    "q-65339": {
      "dom_id": "step-q65339",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},y-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\, & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=(2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-4, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 2)=-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)+∇\\,f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =0+⟨-4,-\\pi ⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,y-2⟩ \\\\  & =-4(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi (y-2) \\\\  & =4\\pi -4x-\\pi y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
