    "q-97877": {
      "dom_id": "step-q97877",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(2,-2,-1)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1)=2,\\,{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)=-4.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$",
      "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(2,-2,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(2,-2,-1)+∇f(2,-2,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =f(2,-2,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{y}(2,-2,-1),{f}_{z}(2,-2,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-2,y+2,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=2.02,$ $y=-1.98,$ and $z=-1.05$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.02,-1.98,-1.05) & ≈4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨2.02-2,-1.98+2,-1.05+1⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨2,-2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.02,-0.05⟩ \\\\  & =4+(0.04-0.04+0.2) \\\\  & =4.2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
